    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d598">

                <pb facs="0640" n="636" sameAs="frus:frus1939v03/pg636"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/14296: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-01-04T15:00:00+00:00">January 4, 1939—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 4—2:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>12. The Chief of the Far Eastern Division of the Foreign Office<anchor n="60"
                        xml:id="d598-f1"/> expressed the view to me last night that Wang’s<anchor
                        n="61" xml:id="d598-f2"/> defection has not weakened Chiang Kai
                        Shek’s<anchor n="62" xml:id="d598-f3"/> position and that the latter is as
                    determined as ever to see it through to the end. His information is that the
                    American credit and hopes of a British credit have greatly encouraged the
                    Chinese and that Chinese morale has definitely improved in the last 2 or 3
                    months.</p>
                <p>Hoppenot said that recently the Japanese Chargé d’Affaires inquired whether
                    France would grant a credit to China. He replied that he knew of no such plan at
                    present. He added to me that if anything of this nature were done it would be
                    through the Bank of Indo-China but that there was nothing definite in mind at
                    present.</p>
                <p>He expressed the view that with the militarists on top in Japan as a result of
                    the Cabinet shake-up the army now would make a drive on Yunnan in the hope of
                    shutting off Chinese supplies by the Burma route which is now operating fairly
                    effectively.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d598-f1" n="60" xml:id="d598-f1-content">
                <p>Reference is to Henri Hoppenot, who, on October 24, 1938, was transferred from
                    the Far Eastern to the European Section as head of Section.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d598-f2" n="61" xml:id="d598-f2-content">
                <p>Wang Ching-wei, formerly Deputy Leader of the Kuomintang (Nationalist Party) at
                    Chungking.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d598-f3" n="62" xml:id="d598-f3-content">
                <p>Chairman of the Chinese Supreme National Defense Council and Leader of the
                    Kuomintang.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
